    faqs: [
      { q: "What is reverse logistics?", a: "Reverse logistics is the movement of goods back from the customer to the seller, covering returns intake, inspection, restocking, relabeling, repacking, and disposition. Delivery Group Inc. manages that backward flow so returned units are recovered instead of written off." },
      { q: "What is the difference between reverse logistics and returns management?", a: "Returns management is the customer-facing and administrative side of handling a return. Reverse logistics is the physical processing of the returned goods: receiving, inspecting, restocking, and disposition. Delivery Group Inc. handles the physical reverse flow and connects it to your inventory." },
      { q: "What does your returns processing include?", a: "It includes intake and receiving against the original order, inspection, restocking sellable units, relabeling and repacking, identifying damaged inventory, and disposition by your rules. Sellable units go back into pickable stock." },
      { q: "Can returned units be resold?", a: "Yes. Units that pass inspection are relabeled or repacked as needed and restocked into sellable inventory. Damaged or unsellable units are identified and flagged for your decision." },
      { q: "Do you handle Amazon FBA returns?", a: "Yes. Delivery Group Inc. processes FBA customer returns and removal orders, including the relabeling and repacking needed to make units compliant and resellable." },
      { q: "Do I need to use you for fulfillment to use your reverse logistics?", a: "No, but there is an advantage if you do. When forward and reverse logistics run on one system, returned units restock with no hand-off to a second vendor, which recovers value faster." },
    ],
